The precise details of how the dynamo effect works are not yet fully understood, but scientists believe that it involves a combination of factors, including the rotation of the Earth, convection within the outer core, and the presence of a seed magnetic field.

As the Earth rotates, the liquid iron in the outer core is set in motion, creating large-scale convection currents. These currents cause the liquid metal to rise and fall, generating electrical currents through the process of induction. The electrical currents, in turn, create a magnetic field that circulates around the Earth.

The presence of a seed magnetic field is also believed to play an important role in the generation of the Earth's magnetic field. This seed field may be generated by the motion of charged particles in the ionosphere or by other means. Once the seed field is established, the dynamo effect amplifies it to create the strong magnetic field that we observe today.

