Re: [Evolution-hackers] Mail Caching


It is generic, most of is even reusable (which i think is what you meant to say), but a lot isn't because it depends very very much on the backend.  IMAP for example can only cache what is downloaded, which may not be everythiblem:
public class Leak {
	
	public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
		go();
		//
		// Take heap dump here...
		//	
		System.in.read();
	}

	private static void go() throws IOException {
		try (Context context = Context.create("js")) {
			context.getBindings("js").putMember("example", new ExampleClass());
			Source s = Source.newBuilder("js", "print(example.toString());", "name").build();
			context.eval(s);
		}
	}
	
}

The heap dump shows a single instance of ExampleClass still hanging around, reachable via a static field in JSRegExp called LAZY_INDEX_PROXY.  Here is a screenshot from VisualVM's view of the heap dump:

It only ever seems to hold onto the first instance of ExampleClass, if i create and bind others then it's still just the first.
For now i'm working around this situation by binding a dummy instance of UserSession when my service starts up, one that doesn't contain any real data and so only leaks a few bytes, but i would like to either understand this case, or report a bug if it's not intended behaviour.


Hi, @stikdragon!
This particular leak should be plugged by RC15. JSRegExp$LazyRegexResultIndexProxyProperty no longer retains the readStartArrayElement node, through which the JavaScript context, along with its global object, was retained for longer than was necessary.
https://github.com/graalvm/graaljs/commit/23517465c69dc3724a24b28f0a324ba8199fd621#diff-58330de66f26048ebbf47673233f8513L1416
https://github.com/graalvm/graaljs/commit/65987cc5ac8b2e948724c0632ed2ca8fd5d32c78#diff-f379913b9e283bc9d96b83abc0b1bf95L120


I checked your reproducer locally and could indeed see the leak with RC13. With RC16, the heap dump revealed no instances of ExampleClass on the heap.


Thanks for your quick response, and i'm glad to hear it's all fixed.  I'm still struggling to get anything after rc13 to work though.
At the risk of going off-topic: Has the way we interact with Strings returned from Java-land changed?  It used to be the case that i could do example.toString().equals(""), but it almost seems as though it's not really a java.lang.String anymore and i get TypeError: (intermediate value).toString(...).equals is not a function



I'm still struggling to get anything after rc13 to work though.

The default configuration of Context become more restrictive after rc13, see https://github.com/oracle/graal/blob/master/sdk/CHANGELOG.md For example, an access to arbitrary fields/methods of Java classes is not allowed by default by now. I guess that you are running into these restrictions. You can restore the original behavior using Context.Builder.allowAllAccess(true).


Ah, i feared something like that might have happened.  I will do some digging elsewhere.  Thanks guys.
Allow downstream modules to implement their own policies for instance principal


Currently, the operator creates a policy to manage all resources in compartment. We want to minimize this and enable downstream modules to create their own policies.
However, we should also allow a setting where the default manage all policy in compartment is still created.


