The way to go seems to be to:
nette has been to show how to apply the gravmagsubs functions to calculate gravitational and magnetic anomalies. Methods of calculating anomalies at discrete points, and across arrays of regularly spaced points, have been demonstrated.

In general the calculation time is rapid (a few seconds) for models with tens of thousands of rectangular prisms and tens of thousands of station locations, when calculated on a scientific laptop (64-bit Intel I9 processor (2.40 GHz) with 64 GB RAM). For gravity, calculation time can be improved by taking advantage of the sensitivity matrix, which allows for rapid calculations of multiple density models given the same set of prisms.

