Building a Meaningful Life from the Bottom Up http://www.thesimpledollar.com/building-a-meaningful-life-from-the-bottom-up/ http://www.thesimpledollar.com/building-a-meaningful-life-from-the-bottom-up/#comments Sat, 21 Jun 2014 14:00:11 +0000 http://www.thesimpledollar.com/?p=24350 One of the biggest struggles of modern life is the search for meaning and purpose. Most of the time, we go through our day-to-day lives just accomplishing the things we need to get done and relishing the spare time that’s left over. It can feel a lot like jogging on a treadmill, where life is ...

The post Building a Meaningful Life from the Bottom Up appeared first on The Simple Dollar.

One of the biggest struggles of modern life is the search for meaning and purpose.

Most of the time, we go through our day-to-day lives just accomplishing the things we need to get done and relishing the spare time that’s left over. It can feel a lot like jogging on a treadmill, where life is running backwards and we’re running just to stay in place and not fall off the back end.

I know that there are been large periods of my life where I have felt this way – and those feelings still pop up from time to time. My to-do list of the tasks I have to accomplish by the end of a given day often feels incredible.

How We Get Sidetracked from Our Life Goals

Take my to-do list for today as an example. I have to get an article finished for The Simple Dollar, another one finished for U.S. News and World Report, and submit a draft of a book chapter, plus I have to take my daughter and oldest son to taekwondo practice, go to a community board meeting, and go to my youngest son’s t-ball game. That doesn’t include housework or parenting requirements.

Completing those things doesn’t seem to get me ahead. It seems to just let me stay in place.

Over time, I often lose track of what the point of all of it is. I keep running in place, but I don’t know why. I feel deeply disenchanted, but I keep running only because I feel like I’m supposed to.

That’s a dangerous place to be in. It’s a place where you’re going to splurge on your discipline. You’re going to make giant spending mistakes. You’re going to end up wasting a lot of time simply because you feel like you should “burn” some time in order to feel in “control” again.

I’ve been there. I’m willing to bet there’s a good chance you’ve been there, too.

There are lots of time management tricks that make a big to-do list more workable, but it doesn’t really help with the overall problem of wondering what the point of all of it is.

My So, and she didn’t know how to shake it.

One day, Ana met a man named Jack. Jack was different from the others that she had met. He didn’t seem to want anything from her. In fact, he didn’t seem to be interested in her power at all. Instead, he talked to her about his dreams, about the things he wanted to accomplish in life. He was kind and patient, and for the first time in a long while, Ana felt like she could talk to someone who would understand her.

For months, Ana and Jack talked about everything under the sun. They talked about their hopes and their fears, about their struggles and their victories. They shared their dreams, both the ones they had at night, and the ones they had for their future. Ana found herself feeling less alone than she had in years.

One night, Ana wandered into Jack’s dreams. She found herself in a dark city, full of empty buildings and streets. Jack was there, and he looked lost. Ana asked him what was wrong, but he didn’t respond. He just kept walking, his head down.

As they walked, Ana realized that the city was a manifestation of Jack’s worries and insecurities. She tried to help him overcome them, hoping that perhaps she could help him in the same way that she had helped so many others. But no matter what she tried, she couldn’t seem to break through to him.

Eventually, they reached a dark alleyway, and Jack stopped. He turned to Ana and looked at her with tear-filled eyes. “I’m sorry,” he said. “I’ve been lying to you.”

Ana was taken aback. “What do you mean?” she asked.

“I’m not who you think I am,” Jack said. “I’ve been using you, using your power. I’m one of those people who wants what you have.”

Ana felt a wave of nausea wash over her. She couldn’t believe what she was hearing. Jack had been her lifeline, her only source of human connection in a world that had been unkind to her. And now, he was telling her that he had been using her all along.

She didn’t know what to do. She was angry, hurt, and confused. Part of her wanted to lash out at Jack, to hurt him in the same way that he had hurt her. But another part of her felt a surge of something else, something that she hadn’t felt in a long time.

It was hope.

Ana realized that, even though Jack had fooled her, he had also shown her that there was a world outside of dreams - a world full of people
