    type: float
  - parameter_name: draw
    description: stino" Target is the temperature you set to engage the fans.</P><P>"Max" is the maximum temperature you want the card to run at under load.</P><P>If you set it as I recommend. "Destino" @45c (default) engages your fans when the temp hits 45c and GRADUALLY INCREASES as needed to keep it at/under the "Max" temperature you set.</P><P>You are telling the fans to not become involved until the temp reaches 75c and keep the temperature under 65c. Crazy.</P><P>You also have not raised the Power Limit...can you translate English? Read this about the Power Limit setting &gt; <A href="https://community.amd.com/thread/195150">AMD graphics performance</A> </P><P></P><P>"Your AMD graphics card has a Power Limit setting in Overdrive. The default setting is 0. That is a power saving (electricity)&nbsp; setting that throttles your card when it begins to draw more power...like when your gaming. Enabling Overdrive and Raising the Power Limit to it's Highest + value (currently +50 or +20 depending on the card) <SPAN style="text-decoration: underline;">Disables</SPAN> this throttling feature. It is not overclocking, it just allows the card to perform to its maximum capabilities."</P><P></P><P>IMO, reinstall the AMD drivers using the clean install method &gt; <A href="https://community.amd.com/thread/180667">Clean Install AMD Graphics Drivers</A> </P><P>Open Wattman and set the temp and power as indicated in the picture. Click 'apply'. Do not touch any other setting. Leave everything else set at default (automatic).</P><P><span class="lia-inline-image-display-wrapper"><img src="https://community.amd.com/skins/images/1FAAE811109F19EAE6DAAF21C21A11C5/responsive_peak/images/image_not_found.png" /></span></P></BODY></HTML></description>
<pubDate>Thu, 11 Jan 2018 16:10:53 GMT</pubDate>
<guid>https://community.amd.com/t5/pc-drivers-software/rx580-fan-speed-doesn-t-decrease-after-gaming/m-p/266529#M79137</guid>
<dc:creator>kingfish</dc:creator>
<dc:date>2018-01-11T16:10:53Z</dc:date>
</item>
<item>
<title>Re: [FAN SPEED] FAN DON'T DECREASE RPMs AFTER CLOSING ANY GAME</title>
<link>https://community.amd.com/t5/pc-drivers-software/rx580-fan-speed-doesn-t-decrease-after-gaming/m-p/266530#M79138</link>
<description><HTML><HEAD></HEAD><BODY><P>EDIT &gt;&gt; After I restore settings and apply TEMP CONFIGURATION in a GAME PERFIL (left Global perfil in default and config temps only in game perfil), works.<BR />After i quit the game, FAN go to ZERO!<BR />Probably that's bug will be resolved only after a OS clean installation</P><P></P><P>XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX</P><P></P><P>I can understand english, but I've difficult to write, maybe I'm not made me understandable.</P><P></P><P>Before the problem appears, although in the BIOS ( I copy BIOS and used Pollaris BIOS Editting to see) the target is 75C,</P><P>in 52C FAN start and 50C FAN stop. How i set the max temp to 65C, the GPU never exceeded 65C~70C.</P><P>that it: 52C FAN ON &gt;&gt; UP to 65~67C, exit game, down to -50C fan OFF</P><P></P><P>Now, When i change target to other value than 75C in RADEON SETTING, FAN settings changes to manual itself, just like the print.</P><P>I try put exactly how you ask, but when i put target = 40, fan setting changes itself to manual. Temp respect the configured limit,</P><P>never exceeded 65C, but after that FAN rotation not go down...</P><P></P><P>See in video : <A href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=owaTM0oHeJs" title="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=owaTM0oHeJs">RADEON SETTINGS _ ERROR - YouTube</A></P><P></P><P>I already try clean instalation with DDU and I used CCLEANER to clean WindownsReg.</P><P>I left the default settings in RADEON SETTINGS</P><P>- Start the GAME, 52C FAN START</P><P>- GPU get 75C.. 80C</P><P>- RPM encreasses</P><P>- exit game</P><P>- RPM remais, even whit GPU temp now in 40C</P><P></P><P></P><P>I Try changes the BIOS ( GPU have DUAL BIOS)</P></BODY></HTML></description>
<pubDate>Thu, 11 Jan 2018 18:08:10 GMT</pubDate>
