Remember to follow the instructions and apply each product in the order listed above for maximum effectiveness.Write a comprehensive guidebook for first-time travelers to Japan, covering topics such as the best time to visit, obtaining visas, currency exchange, transportation options, accommodation choices, must-see tourist destinations, cultural practices and etiquette, local cuisine recommendations, and suggested day trip itineraries. The guidebook should be engaging, informative, and visually appealing, with user-friendly maps, photographs, and anecdotes that capture the essence of Japan.
Welcome to Japan!

As a first-time traveler to this fascinating country, you may have many questions and concerns about what to expect during your visit. This guidebook is designed to help you plan a memorable trip to Japan, covering everything from the best time to visit, obtaining visas, currency exchange, transportation options, accommodation choices, must-see tourist destinations, cultural practices and etiquette, local cuisine recommendations, and suggested day trips.

Best Time to Visit

Japan is a beautiful country that can be enjoyed year-round, but the best time to visit largely depends on your preferences and interests. Spring (March to May) is heralded by the beautiful cherry blossoms, summer (June to August) is perfect for exploring the country's numerous outdoor attractions, autumn (September to November) boasts stunning fall foliage, while winter (December to February) is ideal for winter sports and experiencing traditional winter festivities.

Obtaining Visas

If you are visiting Japan for tourism purposes and do not plan to engage in any paid activities such as work or business, you may not need a visa for stays of up to 90 days. Citizens of certain countries such as the United States, the United Kingdom, and Australia can enter Japan visa-free. However, it's essential to check the visa requirements for your home country before your trip to avoid any complications.

Currency Exchange

The currency used in Japan is the Japanese Yen (JPY), with denominations of 1,000, 5,000, and 10,000 notes. It's essential to exchange currency before you arrive in Japan as it can be challenging to find an exchange center, especially outside major cities. You can exchange currency at international airports, banks, post offices, or Currency Exchange Bureaus.

Transportation Options

Japan is known for its seamless and efficient public transportation network, making it easy to travel around. Some of the transportation options available in Japan include Shinkansen Bullet Trains, local trains, buses, subways, and taxis. The Japan Rail Pass is an excellent option for those planning to travel around the country for a set amount of time.

Accommodation Choices

Japan offers a wide range of accommodation options, from traditional ryokans (Japanese inns) to modern hotels, and capsule hotels. Additionally, tourists can also opt to stay in Airbnb accommodations or youth hostels. It's essential to book accommodation in advance, especially during the peak tourist season when hotels can fill up quickly.

Must-See Tourist Destinations

Japan is a treasure trove of tourist attractions, and some of the must-visit destinations include Tokyo, with its bustling streets, neon lights, and excellent food scene; Kyoto, with its beautiful temples and shrines, and stunning gardens; Hiroshima, which is a poignant reminder of Japan's World War II history; and Nara, which is home to the famous Todaiji Temple and Nara Park, famouUnwrapped { nullable_id: false, other_fields: [RecursivePointer, Builtin(Int(I64))] }), Builtin(Int(I64))], result: Union(NullableUnwrapped { nullable_id: false, other_fields: [RecursivePointer, Builtin(Int(I64))] }) } combo must be in DeclarationToIndex', compiler/mono/src/borrow.rs:227:9

The program works fine when using only one call to single, when inlining the definitions of single or prepend, or when defining prepend as follows:
prepend : LinkedList elem, elem -> LinkedList elem
prepend = \elems, elem -> Prepend elems elem



This no longer panics, but yields an incorrect error message
module []

LinkedList elem : [Empty, Prepend (LinkedList elem) elem]

prepend : LinkedList elem, elem -> LinkedList elem
prepend = Prepend

single : elem -> LinkedList elem
single = \elem -> prepend Empty elem

main =
    nested = single (single 42)
    "Hello, World!\n"

── TYPE MISMATCH in Module.roc ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

This 2nd argument to prepend has an unexpected type:

9│  single = \elem -> prepend Empty elem
                                    ^^^^

This elem value is a:

    elem

But prepend needs its 2nd argument to be:

    elem

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

1 error and 2 warnings found in 79 ms

As above, adding an explicit parameter to the definition of prepend fixes the issue.
reproduce segfault when non-recursive tag union contains recursive tag union


DON'T MERGE ME
This is not a real PR, just a convenient way to send @rtfeldman an SSCCE for a problem in glue generation!
The code in this PR (namely, example/recursive-shenanigans/platform/main.roc) compiles, but causes a segfault at runtime. These are the relevant types:
Below : [Below (List Below)]

Rec : [Rec Below]

As of 1db7c366, running cargo run -- run examples/recursive-shenanigans/main.roc will segfault.


making this a draft so it's less mergeable 👍


The problem is that we're generating a pointer for Rec instead of a repr(transparent) wrapper around a Below. Fixing this is surprisingly tricky, but as a workaround, you can just use Below directly instead of the Rec wrapper (pretending the wrapper type didn't exist at all) and everything works - at least in this small example!
