This is just a basic example of how you can use JavaScript to dynamically generate recipe suggestions for a dish that features both broccoli and chicken. You can customize the code to fit your specific needs and use other APIs or data sources to get more creative recipe ideas. <|endoftext|>

USER: That's really helpful! But what if I want to take it a step further and include recommendations for wine pairings with the dish? Can JavaScript integrate with a wine API to accomplish this?

ASSISTANT: Yes, you can use JavaScript to integrate with a wine API to provide recommendations for wine pairings with the dish. One example of a wine API you can use is the Wine.com API, which provides access to detailed information on over 50,000 different wines. Here's an example of how you can modify the previous code to include wine pairing recommendations:
1. First, sign up for a free account on the Wine.com API website and get an API key.
2. Add an additional input field to your HTML form for the user to enter their preferred type of wine (such as red or white).
```html

Broccoli:
  
  

Chicken:
  
  

Wine:
  
  

Submit

```
3. Modify the fetch() method to make a GET request to both the Spoonacular and Wine.com APIs, passing in the ingredients entered in the form and the preferred wine type. You can also use the Promise.all() method to wait for both requests to finish before processing the data.
```js
const form = document.querySelector('form');
const button = document.querySelector('button');
button.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
 event.preventDefault();
 const broccoli = document.getElementById('broccoli').value;
 const chicken = document.getElementById('chicken').value;
 const wine = document.getElementById('wine').value;
 const spoonacularUrl = `https://api.spoonacular.com/recipes/complexSearch?apiKey=YOUR_API_KEY&query=${broccoli}+${chicken}`;
 const winecomUrl = `https://api.wine.com/api/v1/pairing?food=${broccoli}%20${chicken}&wine=${wine}&apikey=YOUR_API_KEY`;
 Promise.all([
 fetch(spoonacularUrl).then(response => response.json()),
 fetch(winecomUrl).then(response => response.json())
 ]).then(data => {
 const spoonacularData = data[0];
 const winecomData = data[1];
 console.log(spoonacularData);
 console.log(winecomData);
 // Process the data and display it on the page
 }).catch(error => console.error(error));
});
```
4. Once you have the data from both APIs, you can process it to generate recipe and wine pairing suggestions. You can use a similar template literal approach to create HTML cards for each suggestion, including the recipe title, image, ingredients, instructions, and recommended wine pairings.
```js
const results = document.querySelector('.results');
spoonacularData.results.forEach(recipe => {
 const winePairings = winecomData.pairings.map(pairing => `- ${pairing}
`).join('');
 const card = `
 
 ![${recipe.title}](${recipe.image})
${ret. comment:ask.metafilter.com,2007:site.64386-968661 Fri, 08 Jun 2007 16:07:26 -0800 adamrice By: luriete http://ask.metafilter.com/64386/Online-shopping-with-free-return-policies#968673 I've been able to take REI items back years after purchase (REI-brand items, not anything bought there) - including a 10-year-old tent with a zipper that broke.<br> <br> That said, they seem to have drastically changed their policy in the last year, and are almost hostile as far as returns go now, so I'm not sure how much mileage you'll get out of their guarantee. comment:ask.metafilter.com,2007:site.64386-968673 Fri, 08 Jun 2007 16:16:22 -0800 luriete By: Civil_Disobedient http://ask.metafilter.com/64386/Online-shopping-with-free-return-policies#968731 CostCo used to have the <a href="http://bargaineering.com/articles/costcos-incredible-return-policy.html">best return policy of any company, ever</a>. But that <a href="http://www.fatwallet.com/forums/messageview.php?start=0&catid=24&threadid=679223">changed for the worse beginning this year</a>, sadly. Might just be for electronic stuff, though. comment:ask.metafilter.com,2007:site.64386-968731 Fri, 08 Jun 2007 17:31:30 -0800 Civil_Disobedient By: twiggy http://ask.metafilter.com/64386/Online-shopping-with-free-return-policies#968793 I'd note that the reason the CostCo policy changed "for the worse" is because so many people abused the policy.<br> <br> I can't imagine a comprehensive list exists, and frankly it shouldn't. Whether your intention is to use it for Good or Evil(tm), enough people discovering that list would guarantee that just like with CostCo, the scheming evil-users will ruin it for those who just want to be able to legitimately try something out and return it if they're dissatisfied. comment:ask.metafilter.com,2007:site.64386-968793 Fri, 08 Jun 2007 19:21:13 -0800 twiggy By: Joh http://ask.metafilter.com/64386/Online-shopping-with-free-return-policies#968844 Zappos.com, I have personally used their awesome return policy many times. Its impossible to find out if shoes are the correct size without trying them on, so the return policy is a godsend. Free return shipping. However, they do expect the shoes to be returned unused. Instructions explain that you should try the shoes on and walk around on a carpeted surface, to avoid wear on the soles so they are still eligible for return. comment:ask.metafilter.com,2007:site.64386-968844 Fri, 08 Jun 2007 20:16:38 -0800 Joh By: zippy http://ask.metafilter.com/64386/Online-shopping-with-free-return-policies#968953 Nordstrom's stores accept returns no questions asked. I would expect their web site does too. comment:ask.metafilter.com,2007:site.64386-968953 Sat, 09 Jun 2007 00:53:20 -0800 zippy By: sneakin http://ask.metafilter.com/64386/Online-shopping-with-free-return-policies#968998 +1 Zappos comment:ask.metafilter.com,2007:site.64386-968998 Sat, 09 Jun 2007 05:19:41 -0800 sneakin By: santojulieta http://ask.metafilter.com/64386/Online-shopping-with-free-return-policies#969042 It's a local shop to Michigan, but <a href="http://www.gazellesports.com/">Gazelle Sports</a> has a fantastic return policy. I'd bought a pair of Keen shoes there last May, and by September, they were falling apart. I rolled into one of the shops (not the one where I'd initially purchased) and said, "Hey. My shoes are falling apart. I'd like a new pair. I do not have a receipt," and they said, "Surely. Let us get you the newest model of your shoe, and we'll look up your receipt." This may have been partially related to Keen's return policies, but they also take back other shoes as well much after the fact. comment:ask.metafilter.com,2007:site.64386-969042 Sat, 09 Jun 2007 07:07:22 -0800 santojulieta
